WEBVTT

1
00:00:13.302 --> 00:00:14.522
heart pounded with terror!

2
00:00:14.923 --> 00:00:20.366
The horrible squid had Ned pinned to the deck and was about to attack him with its terrible

3
00:00:20.446 --> 00:00:26.069
beak. I rushed to Ned's aid, but Captain Nemo got there before me and slashed at the squid's

4
00:00:26.129 --> 00:00:27.290
tentacles with his hatchet.

5
00:00:28.290 --> 00:00:31.052
Ned broke free and killed the squid with his harpoon.

6
00:00:32.108 --> 00:00:38.410
The battle with the squid lasted fifteen minutes before the monsters, vanquished and mutilated, slid off the Nautilus

7
00:00:38.490 --> 00:00:39.930
and slipped under the waves.

8
00:00:40.950 --> 00:00:45.712
Captain Nemo gazed at the sea that had swallowed up one of his crewmen, and his eyes filled

9
00:00:45.732 --> 00:00:46.372
with tears.

10
00:00:47.432 --> 00:00:53.154
This was the second companion he had lost since our arrival onboard his submarine, but this unfortunate man

11
00:00:53.234 --> 00:00:55.514
would not be buried in the coral cemetery.

12
00:00:56.555 --> 00:01:00.676
Captain Nemo retired to his cabin, and I didn't see him for quite a few days.

13
00:01:02.132 --> 00:01:05.394
However I could tell that he remained sad and indecisive.

14
00:01:06.475 --> 00:01:10.678
He was the heart and soul of the Nautilus, and the vessel mirrored his emotions.

15
00:01:11.659 --> 00:01:15.741
When he was shut up in his cabin, the submarine floated at random upon the waves.

16
00:01:16.782 --> 00:01:21.185
It seemed as if Captain Nemo could not tear himself away from the scene of this last battle,

17
00:01:21.525 --> 00:01:23.767
from this sea that had devoured one of his men.

18
00:01:24.868 --> 00:01:30.752
We passed ten days like this until May 1, when the Nautilus resumed its northerly course.

19
00:01:31.804 --> 00:01:36.845
Now we were following the Gulf Stream, which flows like a river through the Atlantic Ocean.

20
00:01:37.966 --> 00:01:41.827
A week later we were off the coast of North Carolina in the United States.

21
00:01:42.927 --> 00:01:46.868
Once more the vessel seemed to be moving at random with no one in charge.

22
00:01:47.909 --> 00:01:51.650
Under these circumstances, I thought that escape might be possible.

23
00:01:52.990 --> 00:01:56.491
Perhaps we could be picked up by one of the many ships sailing these waters.

24
00:01:57.451 --> 00:02:00.212
There was only one problem: the weather was horrible.

25
00:02:01.248 --> 00:02:06.271
We were in waters where hurricanes are often found, and to attempt an escape in a frail boat

26
00:02:06.611 --> 00:02:08.232
was to risk certain destruction.

27
00:02:09.272 --> 00:02:13.335
Even Ned admitted this, although he didn't give up the idea of escape altogether.

28
00:02:13.355 --> 00:02:20.238
"Professor, this voyage must come to an end," he said to me as we descended from the platform.

29
00:02:20.258 --> 00:02:26.682
"I followed Captain Nemo to the South Pole, but I declare I won't follow him to the North

30
00:02:26.722 --> 00:02:30.644
Pole." "What do you suggest we do since we can't escape right now?"

31
00:02:31.692 --> 00:02:33.613
"We must speak to the captain.

32
00:02:34.473 --> 00:02:38.135
If we continue northward, we will soon be near Canada, my homeland.

33
00:02:38.876 --> 00:02:40.917
It makes me furious just to think about it!"

34
00:02:42.377 --> 00:02:46.159
Ned's moods had grown gloomier, and his face had changed in appearance.

35
00:02:47.240 --> 00:02:50.662
I understood Ned's suffering because I was feeling homesick myself.

36
00:02:51.802 --> 00:02:55.284
Six months had passed without our having any news from land.

37
00:02:56.268 --> 00:03:02.051
The change in Captain Nemo's personality since the battle with the squid had further diminished my enthusiasm for

38
00:03:02.111 --> 00:03:03.372
life aboard the submarine.

39
00:03:03.392 --> 00:03:06.493
"Well?" said Ned when I didn't reply.

40
00:03:06.514 --> 00:03:08.815
"Are you going to speak to the captain or not?

41
00:03:09.195 --> 00:03:12.156
You can tell him that I want to know if he's ever going to set us free."

42
00:03:12.177 --> 00:03:19.480
"He has already told us that will never happen," I reminded Ned, "and besides, I never see the

43
00:03:19.520 --> 00:03:20.241
captain anymore.

44
00:03:20.641 --> 00:03:21.642
He seems to avoid me."

45
00:03:21.662 --> 00:03:26.024
"Then you must go to see him at once," said Ned.

46
00:03:27.092 --> 00:03:32.334
I returned to my cabin, which adjoined Captain Nemo's, and decided to take action before I lost my

47
00:03:32.374 --> 00:03:36.516
nerve. I knocked at his door, but there was no answer.

48
00:03:37.356 --> 00:03:39.277
I knocked again, and then turned the handle.

49
00:03:40.237 --> 00:03:44.639
The door opened, and I entered to find the captain bending over his work at the table.

50
00:03:45.660 --> 00:03:49.701
He raised his head quickly, frowned, and said harshly, "What are you doing here?"

51
00:03:49.721 --> 00:03:51.922
"I need to speak to you, Captain."

52
00:03:51.942 --> 00:03:53.803
"But I'm busy working.

53
00:03:54.243 --> 00:03:55.744
I don't disturb you when you're working.

54
00:03:56.192 --> 00:03:57.472
Shouldn't you show me the same courtesy?"

55
00:03:58.533 --> 00:04:01.274
This reception was not encouraging, but I pressed on.

56
00:04:01.294 --> 00:04:04.835
"Captain, I need to speak to you about an urgent matter."

57
00:04:05.975 --> 00:04:07.595
"What?" he said sarcastically.

58
00:04:07.615 --> 00:04:09.596
"Have you made some new discovery?

59
00:04:10.296 --> 00:04:13.237
Has the sea shown you something that man has never seen before?"

60
00:04:14.277 --> 00:04:19.839
The conversation wasn't going well at all, but before I could reply, he showed me an open manuscript

61
00:04:19.899 --> 00:04:20.479
on his table.

62
00:04:20.499 --> 00:04:27.688
"Here, Professor Aronnax, is a manuscript that summarizes all my studies of the sea.

63
00:04:28.749 --> 00:04:32.252
It's written in several languages, and it will not perish with me.

64
00:04:33.273 --> 00:04:40.079
This manuscript, accompanied by my real name and my life story, will be enclosed in a small unsinkable

65
00:04:40.119 --> 00:04:46.705
capsule. The last survivor of those men onboard the Nautilus will throw the capsule into the sea, and

66
00:04:46.745 --> 00:04:49.148
the water will carry it to some distant shore."

67
00:04:50.336 --> 00:04:54.037
So he was going to reveal his name and his mysterious past!

68
00:04:54.858 --> 00:04:56.378
That would be a book worth reading.

69
00:04:56.398 --> 00:04:58.959
"Captain," I tried again.

70
00:04:58.979 --> 00:05:02.440
"I appreciate that you want the world to read your manuscript.

71
00:05:03.160 --> 00:05:08.522
The results of your studies must not be lost, but who knows where the wind and waves will

72
00:05:08.562 --> 00:05:09.162
take your book?

73
00:05:09.602 --> 00:05:12.943
Surely you or one of your—" "Never, Professor!"

74
00:05:13.684 --> 00:05:14.344
he interrupted me.

75
00:05:15.732 --> 00:05:22.236
"Perhaps my companions and I could keep it for you, if you set us free and we—" "Set

76
00:05:22.276 --> 00:05:22.776
you free?"

77
00:05:23.196 --> 00:05:24.617
The captain stood up and faced me.

78
00:05:24.637 --> 00:05:28.259
"Yes, sir, that's what I want to talk to you about.

79
00:05:29.220 --> 00:05:33.582
We've been onboard the Nautilus for six months, and we need to know if you intend to keep

80
00:05:33.642 --> 00:05:34.283
us here forever."

81
00:05:34.303 --> 00:05:38.345
"Professor, I'll tell you the same thing I told you six months ago.

82
00:05:38.745 --> 00:05:41.447
Whoever boards this submarine must never leave it."

83
00:05:41.867 --> 00:05:43.188
Captain Nemo crossed his arms.

84
00:05:44.244 --> 00:05:47.926
"I don't speak only for myself, but also for my companions.

85
00:05:48.827 --> 00:05:50.568
Ned is nearly at the end of his rope.

86
00:05:51.568 --> 00:05:55.350
I don't know what he'll do if you keep him here much longer, but I know he's desperate."

87
00:05:55.370 --> 00:05:58.452
"What does it matter to me what Ned thinks or does?"

88
00:05:58.852 --> 00:05:59.433
said the captain.

89
00:05:59.453 --> 00:06:04.035
"Now, Professor, let this be the last time we discuss this matter."

90
00:06:05.556 --> 00:06:07.297
I turned and left the captain's quarters.

91
00:06:08.078 --> 00:06:12.140
I knew our situation was hopeless and I had to tell my companions what I'd just heard.

92
00:06:13.224 --> 00:06:17.605
"Now we know that we can't expect anything from that man," said Ned after he'd heard my story.

93
00:06:17.625 --> 00:06:22.647
"The Nautilus is nearing Long Island, and we'll escape no matter what the weather may be."

94
00:06:23.667 --> 00:06:27.948
But the weather became more and more threatening until a hurricane hit us a few days later.

95
00:06:28.948 --> 00:06:33.269
I thought the captain would take the Nautilus underwater, but he insisted on remaining on the surface.

96
00:06:34.290 --> 00:06:37.631
I went up to the platform and found that he had tied himself to the railing to keep

97
00:06:37.671 --> 00:06:39.791
from being swept overboard by the raging sea.

98
00:06:40.831 --> 00:06:42.492
The sky was streaked with lightning.

99
00:06:43.444 --> 00:06:47.689
I could not bear the brightness of it, but the captain looked right at it and seemed to

100
00:06:47.870 --> 00:06:49.271
envy the spirit of the storm.

101
00:06:50.333 --> 00:06:55.759
The submarine tilted dreadfully, raising its bow into the air as thunder and lightning struck around us.
